      "title": "[New Atlas] Smart scope's spinning sensor fights Earth's spin to erase star trails",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=newatlas.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=newatlas.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<p><img alt=\"Portable enough to bring camping, without giving up real optical power\" src=\"https://assets.newatlas.com/dims4/default/405977a/2147483647/strip/true/crop/6312x4208+0+0/resize/1440x960!/format/webp/quality/90/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnewatlas-brightspot.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com%2F6d%2F34%2F36d3c9fc474587726fd541e69256%2Frakker-dwarflab-day-5.jpg\" /><p>Draco, DwarfLab's new smart telescope, solves deep-sky astrophotography's oldest headache with a sensor that physically spins to cancel out star trails caused by Earth's rotation. It needs no equatorial mount, no guide scope, and no polar alignment. Just a bigger 90-mm lens and a bet that raw aperture beats miniaturization.</p><p><a href=\"https://newatlas.com/consumer-tech/dwarflab-draco-smart-telescope/\">Continue Reading</a></p><p><b>Category:</b> <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/consumer-tech/\">Consumer Tech</a>, <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/technology/\">Technology</a></p><p><b>Tags:</b> <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/tag/telescope/\" rel=\"tag\">Telescope</a>, <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/tag/smart/\" rel=\"tag\">Smart</a>, <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/tag/astronomy/\" rel=\"tag\">Astronomy</a>, <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/tag/amateur-astronomy/\" rel=\"tag\">Amateur astronomy</a>, <a href=\"https://newatlas.com/tag/astrophotography/\" rel=\"tag\">Astrophotography</a></p>",
